854fe1fd Buffer state per draw and render pass restarts: an identical vkCmdBindDescriptorSets changes nothing and a changed one invalidates only the sets and implicit buffers (BufferSize, DynamicOffset, Swizzle) of the stages that changed; unchanged push constants are skipped and changed ones invalidate only the stages in stageFlags; zero-divisor vertex buffers and emulated depth bounds bind through the checked binders (the zero-divisor path left the binding cache stale); host-read memory barriers return at once on unified memory; temporary buffers (including vkCmdUpdateBuffer's) return to their pool from one completion handler per command buffer without setPurgeableState toggles, and fills of four equal bytes use a blit fill; heaps are made resident and resources placed in them skip per-resource residency adds and removes, the rest commit once before each Metal command buffer, and removing a buffer from its memory is O(1); imported host memory is untracked when a residency set is in use; and the indirect-count conversions of a render pass's draws run in one compute encoder before the Metal pass starts instead of ending and restarting it per draw (MVK_CONFIG_HOIST_DRAW_CONVERSIONS=0 turns it off). Targeted Vulkan CTS subsets (copies and fills, push constants, indirect draws, mesh draws, memory, dynamic descriptors) match the unmodified build.
ef956862 The binary archive has a queue of its own: its adds (a compile each) ran on the implicit pipeline cache's queue, and a game's thousands of them kept the cache from being written for as long as it compiled (God of War Ragnarok: nothing in 3 minutes); adds still waiting when the device goes are dropped instead of holding up its destruction
a0fa30f5 Pipeline caches keep their work across launches and rebuilds: the implicit cache is also written once a minute while pipelines keep arriving (a game closed, crashed or killed during a long precompile saved nothing, and the next launch compiled every shader again; MVK_CONFIG_PIPELINE_CACHE_SAVE_INTERVAL), it and the pipeline cache UUID are keyed on a hash of the sources that decide what a cache holds when the build gives one (MVK_PIPELINE_CACHE_SOURCES_HASH, scripts/moltenvk-cache-key.sh) instead of the build UUID that every rebuild changed, each library keeps its compiled metallib next to its MSL (Metal's private libraryDataContents), loaded instead of running Metal's front end (God of War Ragnarok's kernels: 187 ms from MSL, 0.03 ms from the metallib; MVK_CONFIG_CACHE_COMPILED_SHADERS=0 keeps only the MSL), and compiled pipelines go into a device-wide MTLBinaryArchive next to the cache, looked up first with MTLPipelineOptionFailOnBinaryArchiveMiss so a relaunch skips Metal's back end (MVK_CONFIG_BINARY_ARCHIVE=0 disables it)
37499e09 The Cereal size check expects the padding CompilerMSL::Options and the structs holding it have now (3 bytes more), so it no longer reports them incomplete at every start
900cb052 MVK_CONFIG_DGC_STATS=1 logs, every 2000 vkCmdExecuteGeneratedCommandsEXT() calls, how many sequences they allowed and whether a count buffer limited them: what an application's GPU-driven work looks like
0824f472 The application's dispatches run concurrently, as Vulkan lets them: a pipeline barrier between dispatches is already a memory barrier in the compute encoder and any other ends it, and in a serial encoder each dispatch waited for the previous one, leaving the GPU idle between small dispatches (d3d12_perf_probe, 20 compute ExecuteIndirects a frame: GPU 38.4 -> 5.8 ms at 1024 allowed, 64 counted; 4.5 -> 2.1 ms at 64). MVK_CONFIG_CONCURRENT_DISPATCH=0 makes them serial
67a03435 Fragment outputs are converted to their color attachment's kind of number (SPIRV-Cross frag_output_base_types, from the rendering formats): a float output to an integer attachment failed pipeline creation ("output of type float4 is not compatible with a MTLPixelFormatR32Uint color attachment"; VKD3D-Proton's test_render_target_support_validation, an R32_TYPELESS render target)
593fa2cc An image view made through a memory alias (a 2D view of a 3D texture, a block texel view) remembers the image texture it was made from: it is a view of the alias, so its parents never matched the image's texture and the view was released and made again at every use (each render pass's attachments), Metal's texture release waiting on its resource list lock: 38% of God of War Ragnarok's queue encoding time
fbbc4f52 A graphics pipeline's fragment shader library compiles on another thread while its vertex shader converts and compiles: its configuration is made by the same steps (setVertexShaderConfig(), setFragmentShaderConfig(), split out of the add*ShaderToPipeline() functions) and the fragment stage then finds it in the pipeline cache; not for caches the application synchronizes itself. MVK_CONFIG_PARALLEL_STAGE_COMPILE=0 compiles the stages one after the other
f207ae66 Emulated depth bounds tests take their depth snapshot texture from the device, one per queue and kind of depth attachment, which Metal orders between the queue's command buffers: a texture made at every command buffer and every change of depth target took a third of the queue's encoding time in God of War Ragnarok (Metal's resource list lock)
9d3ab08a Shaders are compiled optimized for size (MVK_CONFIG_SHADER_OPTIMIZATION=0 restores Metal's default level): the Metal front end takes about half the time on large shaders (God of War Ragnarok's 1472-line compute shader: 1.07 s, 0.49 s), with the GPU compiler still optimizing behind it; GPU times in the D3D12 probe's shader-heavy scenes are the same
4374d987 The implicit pipeline cache is saved once pipelines stop arriving, in one pass under one hold of the cache's lock: it was rewritten every 3 s while a game compiled (200 MB, a core, and the compile threads waiting on the lock), and a library added between taking the size and writing left the size 0 (VK_INCOMPLETE, "Failed to write 4 bytes to output stream"), also for vkGetPipelineCacheData
8f7f440b Variable-count uniform and storage buffer bindings (BufferAuxSize, sizes kept by binding in the auxiliary buffer) are declared open-ended too: Metal walked VKD3D-Proton's 1,000,000-element SSBO and CBV heap arrays at every pipeline creation, most of its cost in God of War Ragnarok
d8746a56 Texel buffer alignment of a format Metal has no pixel format for (VK_FORMAT_R32G32B32_SFLOAT, D3D12 typed buffers) is the device's limit: Metal asserted and ended the process (God of War Ragnarok on VKD3D-Proton); the view is refused with VK_ERROR_FORMAT_NOT_SUPPORTED as before
7189d350 Variable-count argument buffer bindings are declared open-ended in shaders (one element, indexed past, same layout), so Metal doesn't walk 1,000,000-element arrays at every pipeline creation (~200 ms per pipeline with VKD3D-Proton's descriptor heaps)
7afa2158 Push descriptors keep descriptor info copies in the command (no allocation per push) and recording leaves shared pipeline layout reference counts alone when unchanged; optional parallel encoding of a submission's command buffers (MVK_CONFIG_PARALLEL_SUBMIT_ENCODING, off: slower in the D3D12 probe)
939d8d02 Graphics pipelines: reflect shaders through the resolved modules, so stages with an inline VkShaderModuleCreateInfo (maintenance5, as DXVK 2.7 passes them) don't dereference a null module
333fd01f pipelineCacheUUID: last 4 bytes from the binary's build UUID, so caches kept by apps don't outlive a build
